A new whiteboard is being moved into a classroom. The whiteboard must be taken from the entrance, through the school's corridors and into the classroom. Two of the corridors are perpendicular to each other. The first corridor is 3 metres wide and the second corridor is 4 metres wide, as shown in the diagram. The length of the whiteboard is shown using $L$.

\exfigure{1612_q31_1}

The whiteboard makes an angle $\theta$ to the horizontal on the corner of the corridors such that $0^\circ < \theta < 90^\circ$.

solutions.latex:
The whiteboard turns the corner only if its length is no more than the shortest $L$ over all angles. Entering the corridors, $\theta$ starts near $90^\circ$ and decreases towards $0^\circ$ as the board moves round the corner.

Entering the corridors:

\exfigure{1612_q31_s2}

Moving around the corner:

\exfigure{1612_q31_s3}

As $\theta \to 90^\circ$ or $\theta \to 0^\circ$, $L \to \infty$, but a board that long cannot turn the corner.

\exfigure{1612_q31_s4}

At the critical angle the board just touches the inner corner, and this gives the shortest possible $L$. So the longest board that can be carried round the corner has length equal to the minimum of $L$, which occurs at $\theta = 47.74^\circ$ from part (b).
$$\begin{aligned} L &= \dfrac{3}{\cos 47.74^\circ} + \dfrac{4}{\sin 47.74^\circ} \\ &= 9.8657\ldots \\ &\approx 9.87 \end{aligned}$$
$$\therefore\ \text{the longest possible length is } 9.87 \text{ metres}$$

